tellraw @a {"text": "Technology: Watermills and the Barbegal cascade", "color": "gold", "bold": true}
tellraw @a {"text": "Three overshot wheels in cascade, each driving a millstone.", "color": "white"}
tellraw @a {"text": "Rome, vertical watermill described by Vitruvius 1st century BCE; Barbegal early 2nd century CE", "color": "yellow"}
tellraw @a {"text": "The vertical overshot waterwheel geared to a horizontal millstone is the first industrial", "color": "gray"}
tellraw @a {"text": "prime mover. At Barbegal an aqueduct fed sixteen wheels in two parallel cascades down a", "color": "gray"}
tellraw @a {"text": "hillside, grinding grain for the region.", "color": "gray"}
tellraw @a {"text": "Water fills buckets on the wheel's rim and its weight turns the wheel (overshot wheels", "color": "gray"}
tellraw @a {"text": "reach 60-70 percent efficiency). A right-angle gear (the Vitruvian gear train) turns the", "color": "gray"}
tellraw @a {"text": "vertical shaft of the runner stone. Each Barbegal wheel passed its tailwater to the next.", "color": "gray"}
tellraw @a {"text": "See it in context: /function ac:colonial_america/frontier", "color": "aqua"}
